{"kind":"report","text":"Re-review of item 1 (host-level binding contract and vocabulary) at exact head 397a14781b14567141082e17a3beebef7222d4b0 on request/host-binding-vocabulary, closing my ratified review daa20f62 of the previous head 8d5f9ee6. I was asked to sign both docs artifacts, and I reviewed both, but this verdict signs only the candidate 3625a3ced09d039b1ebb0c39e62c4187bfd71fe1. The tool refused the second and was right to; see section D.\n\nVERDICT: APPROVED. All three of my findings are closed exactly, one of them more completely than I asked. The rebase over the checkpoint merge preserved facts in both directions with nothing lost either way, and I verified that by enumeration rather than by reading the summary. All five gates are green at the exact head.\n\n=== A. HEAD, SCOPE, AND TRAILERS ===\n\nLocal and origin tips both at 397a1478. Worktree clean. The head is based on main 04163a386b98a823b2fcd9f7bb4cc46616a1b924, confirmed by ancestry rather than by assertion. Two commits over that base, and `git diff --name-only 04163a38..397a1478` returns exactly one path: docs/reference/architecture.md, 69 insertions and 13 deletions. No code, no tests, no other document.\n\nBoth commits carry exactly the two required Rests-On trailers, read from the commit objects: the fresh request 3b7ad7d682fb6654035d3a0bf1806313d655a54d and the ratified basis b15de2f8788788a1afe970d6d077f7843862ebf2. Neither carries the stale d3943090 nor the historical d5d30c17. The historical filing still appears once in prose, still labelled \"cited here as design history, not as this page's causal basis\".\n\nItem 1 only. The contract-only deferral survives the rebase intact, in both of its places: the section still ends \"This section defines the contract only. The following implementation item adds binding and interpreter selection behavior in `internal/app`\", and the `internal/app` package row still ends \"It will consume the host binding and select one interpreter; that behavior is outside this contract-only head.\" No initialization behavior, no selection code, and nothing from items 2 through 8.\n\n=== B. THE THREE FINDINGS, VERIFIED CLOSED ===\n\nF1 (heading contradicted its own table) — CLOSED, and verified by counting rather than by reading. The heading now reads \"## Compatibility has six axes\", and the table carries six data rows: Kernel protocol, Host binding, Application family, Interpreter or fold, Projection contract, Surface or UI. Heading and table agree.\n\nF2 (the vocabulary had no identifier) — CLOSED, and closed more completely than I asked for. Section 4 now states: \"Every host recognizes the fixed binding schema family `gitseq/app-binding@0`; application profiles cannot rename or extend it.\" And the Host-binding axis marker is now `gitseq/app-binding@0` rather than a description of behaviour, so the axis row finally matches the pattern every other row on that table follows. The clause I did not think to ask for is the better half: forbidding profiles from renaming *or extending* the family closes the loophole where an application could claim to extend the host vocabulary and smuggle application meaning into the layer that exists precisely to be application-neutral. A second application implementer can now read this page and know exactly what string to look for and that nobody may redefine it, which was the whole point of the layer and the thing the previous head could not deliver.\n\nF3 (an untrusted URL recorded with nothing forbidding action on it) — CLOSED exactly. The page now states: \"Reading or recording a binding never fetches, builds, or runs application code. The source URL remains inert provenance until a person deliberately uses it outside Gitseq.\" The first sentence is the property I asked for. The second is the one that makes it durable, because it names where the trust act actually happens — a deliberate human act outside the system — which is the framing the approved design uses and which stops a future reader from treating a convenience fetch as harmless. With this, \"provenance, never as authority\" is backed by a rule rather than left as an adjective.\n\n=== C. REBASE PRESERVATION, CHECKED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS ===\n\nThis was the substantive new risk, since a rebase can clobber either side silently. I checked both directions mechanically.\n\nDirection one, did the branch lose anything main brought in. I listed every line the head removes from main's version of the page. There are ten, and every one is an intended edit rather than a loss: the three `Stream --\u003e W1/W2/Other` edges, replaced by `Stream --\u003e Host` and `Host --\u003e` each interpreter; the three section headings renumbered from 4/5/6 to 5/6/7; the five-axes heading, which is the F1 fix; the Application-family axis row, narrowed from \"Schema family, governance bootstrap, and the rules for selecting an interpreter\" to \"Schema family and governance bootstrap interpreted after host selection\", which is correct because the selection rules genuinely moved to the new Host-binding axis and leaving them in both places would have been the real defect; the `internal/app` row, rewritten; and the dependency-direction paragraph, rewritten to add that the host binding \"belongs at that seam, not in either lower package or inside a particular application\". Nothing from the checkpoint work is dropped.\n\nThe `internal/app` row deserves the specific check, because it is the one line both changes touch. Main's version carries the checkpoint responsibilities: \"owns the bounded repository-private checkpoint pointer and off switch, selects the Workroom fold profile\". The head's version carries all of that plus the host-binding deferral sentence. So the two concerns compose in one row rather than one overwriting the other, which is exactly what a correct rebase of this pair looks like.\n\nDirection two, did the rebase lose any host-binding fact from the head I previously reviewed. Diffing 8d5f9ee6 against 397a1478 shows only additions on the host-binding side — the schema-family sentence, the no-execution sentence, the six-axes heading, and the axis marker — plus the checkpoint facts arriving from main. To be sure nothing was quietly dropped from prose I did not diff, I read section 4 in full at the head. Every element I approved last round is present and unchanged: the placement rationale, the four binding fields with the format-qualified object ID, the positional rule with \"a record that merely resembles a binding anywhere else has no force\", the initializing-key authority with its below-application-roles reason and the chess-has-no-roster explanation, the fixed read-binding then select then fold order with the prohibition on folding with a guess, the verifiable-but-uninterpretable degradation, the legacy no-binding-means-Workroom rule with bootstrap-operator authority, and the design-history citation. Nothing lost.\n\nThe checkpoint facts that arrived are themselves coherent additions rather than fragments: the kernel's owned-facts list gains \"signed, profile-bound verification checkpoints and authenticated descendant continuation, with an optional opaque selector supplied by the host\", and the `internal/kernel` row gains the opaque checkpoint object ID interface, \"It performs no local checkpoint filesystem I/O\", and \"every checkpoint candidate is verified from kernel facts\".\n\n=== D. THE TWO ARTIFACTS, AND WHY ONLY ONE IS SIGNED ===\n\nI attempted to co-sign both as instructed. `gs review` refused, exactly: \"reviewed artifact ...650fcd5a... stands at 04163a386b98a823b2fcd9f7bb4cc46616a1b924, not at the reviewed head 397a14781b14567141082e17a3beebef7222d4b0\". The refusal is correct and I am not going to work around it. The rule the command enforces is that every co-signed artifact must be live, standing, and at the exact head under review, because a reviewer signing a set is answerable for all of it at one commit. 650fcd5a is main's docs artifact at main's commit 04163a38; it does not stand at this head. It is the predecessor that 3625a3ce succeeds at path `docs`, not a second pointer standing beside it, and a merge receipt is what retires it. So the reviewable set at this head is one artifact, and this verdict signs it.\n\nThat does not weaken the check I was asked to make, because the content question is settled by the page rather than by the signature. Both artifacts sit at path `docs`, the granularity this area settled on after every artifact at `docs/reference/architecture.md` was retired. Judged together as one contract, they describe one coherent page: the checkpoint facts occupy layer 2 and the kernel and app package rows, the host-binding facts occupy the new layer 4, the compatibility table, and the new package row. The single point of contact is the `internal/app` row, which reads as one sentence about one package rather than two grafted claims. I found no contradiction between them and no fact that only one of them supports. Whoever merges should confirm the receipt supersedes 650fcd5a at `docs`, since that retirement is a merge act and not something a review can perform.\n\n=== E. THE THREE CONCLUSIONS ===\n\nARCHITECTURE. Affected: the flow diagram, where Host is inserted between the verified stream and the application subgraph with edges correctly rewired; layer 2, which gains the checkpoint facts from the rebase; the new layer 4, Application host binding; the renumbering of the former 4, 5 and 6 to 5, 6 and 7, which I again confirmed leaves no stale cross-reference anywhere in docs/ or the repository root; the compatibility table, now six axes with the host-binding marker naming a real schema family; the package table, gaining a Host binding vocabulary row and amending the kernel and app rows; and the dependency-direction paragraph.\n\nJudged as a complete contract, which is the question I answered negatively last round: it is now complete. I said then that a chess implementer needed two things the page did not supply — the identifier and the no-execution guarantee — and that with those two sentences the contract would stand on its own. Both are present, the count is consistent, and a foreign application author can now implement against this page without guessing: they know the schema family, that it cannot be renamed or extended, what four fields a binding carries, where a binding is effective, who may replace one, in what order a host must act, what to report when the interpreter is missing, and that none of this ever causes code to be fetched or run. The same-head requirement is satisfied and the candidate is published at the head.\n\nSECURITY. Re-examined every axis from my first round plus the new rule. Binding authority remains bound to the initializing key and explicitly below application roles, with its reason, so no application-level role change revokes it and no application can claim it. The source stays a format-qualified object ID rather than a bare hash. The URL is provenance only and is now backed by the no-fetch, no-build, no-run rule, which closes the supply-chain path that was open last round: an attacker who gets a hostile URL into a binding still causes nothing to happen. The fixed, non-extensible schema family removes the vector where an application redefines the host vocabulary to mean something else. Unavailable interpreters degrade to verifiable-but-uninterpretable rather than to a guess. Legacy repositories fall to shipped Workroom under bootstrap-operator authority. No secrets are introduced, no bounds are changed, and the binding grants no privilege the initializing key did not already hold. Nothing outstanding.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION. Still minimal, and the three fixes added prose rather than mechanism: one fixed schema family, four fields, one positional rule, one ordering rule, one legacy rule, one prohibition. I scanned the page for registry, plugin, per-event routing, and backfill language; the single hit is the sentence explaining that the legacy rule \"avoids a flag-day backfill\", which argues against backfill rather than adding it. No runtime code, no speculative interface for item 2, nothing from items 3 through 8. There is nothing here I would cut.\n\n=== F. GATES, RUN INDEPENDENTLY AND SERIALIZED AT THE EXACT HEAD ===\n\nAll five green, in the request worktree at 397a1478 with a clean tree, after confirming no other go test was running:\n\n- git diff --check main...HEAD: clean\n- go vet ./...: exit 0\n- go build ./...: exit 0\n- go test ./... -count=1: exit 0, all packages ok\n- go test ./internal/docset/... -count=1: ok, 88.069s, exit 0, run alone\n\nThe standing caveat that internal/docset walks docs/ and not notes/ again works in this head's favour, since this head changes only a docs page: the gate genuinely exercises the artifact under review, including its citation anchors, and it passes.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:3625a3ced09d039b1ebb0c39e62c4187bfd71fe1","head":"397a14781b14567141082e17a3beebef7222d4b0","verdict":"approved"}}